// Prefix truncation (DeleteThrough) reclaims the oldest whole segments
// once the caller no longer needs their history.
//
// Truncation is deliberately conservative: it never rewrites entries and
// never splits a batch or a group across the retention boundary. A call
// deletes only complete segments forming a prefix of the on-disk segment
// order whose every committed batch has LastSeq <= seq. A segment that
// ends at the boundary (seq lands in its middle) is kept whole, so one
// less segment may be reclaimed than the caller hinted at.
//
// Crash safety follows the same authoritative-marker discipline as the
// index sidecar, one level up. A tiny file "truncate.idx" records the
// truncation point and the reserved-sequence frontier. Unlike index.idx
// it is not derived: it is the durable record of which history the
// caller chose to discard, and it is read first on every open. Deletion
// is committed in this order:
